#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a185b05c-242a-58bb-9c5d-e94e51c15e77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "1894cde0-6e6d-51ad-a739-34254f787898" :question/math-academy-id "e-10250" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2f034132460f2a6b3a26eb52bd4129fb13ea5a871d766815b83047217b97b3b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-2,5),$ and $f'(x) \\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{4, -4\\} = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = -4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_{\\text{min}} = 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b321acb7-1241-5c20-b307-21d18764ef2c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "73bb5c40-4aa3-57a7-bb63-8be5275bc1f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-444" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,9].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3,$ $5,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,9]$ given that $f(1)=5.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86baf9a7cddb9ea44de15d5c87ba01a1b8d68eb233950fb5f22f73ae4dd661b.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (1,9),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=3$ and $x=6.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,3,6,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+3 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ f(6) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+3-5 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+3-5+4 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have \n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{5, 8, 3, 7\\} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = 3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_{\\text{min}} = 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9187b2a-8c34-5dda-8ae4-2c861f1c0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "41d966eb-f45c-5a8c-9d75-5ad6dfcd202d" :question/math-academy-id "e-445" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0cac30d21bdd2f2cc3aeaa0ed907d6eeb9ff78b3dae92cad40b56fab76bd7237.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $f(-4)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,2),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x = -4, 0 , 2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of two trapezoids and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/a6/a676f3c026bdae86c6c353c3c5a38f6922dfe5b7dc83d955c1ee2d392d9f2021.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =0 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =0-7 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =0-7+1+1.5 \\\\  & =-4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{ 0, -7, -4.5 \\} = -7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{min}} = -7,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b321acb7-1241-5c20-b307-21d18764ef2c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5495561b-b8e3-5cea-ba85-ba6d6ddebae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512289} {:answer/id #uuid "567d486e-aad4-556e-b6f9-b033c500c0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512295} {:answer/id #uuid "9a15af2c-3fdb-5eb8-9d83-be8b4bab64ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512462} {:answer/id #uuid "d97dcea9-edb6-5328-b0e6-580c59c6fd22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512618} {:answer/id #uuid "ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512680} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6416470-1d7a-5a75-a4b7-d19f68263b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-104255" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c23b2d07d656695feda6ae6e92d192661e30f2738b6af8342813b44f2885667.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $6,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a185b05c-242a-58bb-9c5d-e94e51c15e77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3c22357-e423-53dd-881d-10a8851f96ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098350} {:answer/id #uuid "1d473754-30a3-5220-8b02-f868f25d294b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098351} {:answer/id #uuid "215c2d09-dec4-5245-9539-d5cdc275185a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098352} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8e1f41-64f8-59e0-9724-a1c5c1048ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098353} {:answer/id #uuid "5577da54-f5fb-54a7-9f4e-6e9bcb0caab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d473754-30a3-5220-8b02-f868f25d294b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098351} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1e9bdb-d524-5760-8ec4-cba71941a400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9dcfbb8-b458-5675-b01f-0a851451c52c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144824" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4a238887e789979e276421850330c5b36a82c485312c06e831c0df96266b85b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(-1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,7\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9187b2a-8c34-5dda-8ae4-2c861f1c0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59866eb1-6270-5d69-87ee-cd8defdbb03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512309} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f7fac7-0bab-58a8-b469-4851316d7779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512472} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c7e36a-3c3b-5caf-a88f-dad913ccfea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512602} {:answer/id #uuid "e32cfebd-6726-5a8f-8ae6-17f8a1288601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512643} {:answer/id #uuid "e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512649} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63c5698-714c-5871-9be3-b86baeca70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-19083" :question/problem "![](images/42/429cba100e1cebd4a2048226f3c9d252fdec171b33679ab8ff6ba21014386aa4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9187b2a-8c34-5dda-8ae4-2c861f1c0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512471} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9e9bfc-789a-50b5-b6bd-12951e72a53e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" :question/math-academy-id "q-341256" :question/problem "![](images/91/91441cecdbe477e4cf0115c1f7c4cf413fa36786690ff7e025ac2e62c711f66e.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ if $h(-2)=6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a185b05c-242a-58bb-9c5d-e94e51c15e77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512206} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2cc657-19ea-5669-92aa-e552ae923d2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342898" :question/problem "![](images/19/192a69596e851fa35b02bbfd6bda06f01814d0d1c05b5698a4179d00a4bb14d2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b321acb7-1241-5c20-b307-21d18764ef2c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512450} :answer-field/id #uuid "8586adde-a0fb-5449-942e-6459137f3b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342899" :question/problem "![](images/98/98290071482fa2a8082932304fbb0d0cc0db54c47324479da7979079e9542551.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $9$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]$ given that $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9187b2a-8c34-5dda-8ae4-2c861f1c0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512135} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3887f5c-297a-596e-b84b-b76944fcf812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" :question/math-academy-id "q-342944" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab8e0cbaf83156609db79ec08202c8f77acfebd03663dfe273e4d75f9db03afb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ if $f(0)=4.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$"}]}
